<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-trigonometric-ratios-beyond-acute-angles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Trigonometric Ratios Beyond Acute Angles</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurement and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.5 Trigonometry II
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>
                        Determine trigonometric ratios of angles greater than 90 degrees in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery (Ben)</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    12 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To discover, through a paired game, that the signs of the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-coordinates on the unit circle follow a clear pattern depending on the quadrant.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Circulate and listen to learners' reasoning about which quadrant an angle falls in. Do not yet mention <m>\cos</m>, <m>\sin</m>, or CAST<mdash/>let learners build the sign table from coordinates first.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="quadrant-game">
        <title>The Quadrant Game (Ben)</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Play this game in pairs.
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Rounds.</alert> Player A calls out an angle between <m>0°</m> and <m>360°</m>. Player B must say: (i) which quadrant the angle is in; (ii) whether the <m>x</m>-coordinate of the corresponding point on the unit circle is positive or negative; (iii) whether the <m>y</m>-coordinate is positive or negative. Swap roles and repeat. Play at least four rounds each.
                        </p>
                        <note component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Useful example angles to suggest: <m>30°</m>, <m>150°</m>, <m>200°</m>, <m>310°</m>. Prompt learners who are struggling to sketch a set of axes and mark where the angle lands. Remind them that the unit circle has radius 1, so every point on it is 1 unit from the origin.
                            </p>
                        </note>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            After your rounds, fill in the summary table below. For each quadrant, record the sign (<m>+</m> or <m>-</m>) of the <m>x</m>-coordinate and the <m>y</m>-coordinate of any point on the unit circle in that quadrant.
                        </p>
                        <table xml:id="quadrant-sign-table">
                            <title>Signs of coordinates by quadrant</title>
                            <tabular halign="center">
                                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                                    <cell>Quadrant</cell>
                                    <cell>Angles</cell>
                                    <cell>Sign of <m>x</m></cell>
                                    <cell>Sign of <m>y</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q1</cell>
                                    <cell><m>0°</m> to <m>90°</m></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q2</cell>
                                    <cell><m>90°</m> to <m>180°</m></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q3</cell>
                                    <cell><m>180°</m> to <m>270°</m></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q4</cell>
                                    <cell><m>270°</m> to <m>360°</m></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                    <cell></cell>
                                </row>
                            </tabular>
                        </table>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <tabular halign="center">
                                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                                    <cell>Quadrant</cell>
                                    <cell>Angles</cell>
                                    <cell>Sign of <m>x</m></cell>
                                    <cell>Sign of <m>y</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q1</cell>
                                    <cell><m>0°</m> to <m>90°</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q2</cell>
                                    <cell><m>90°</m> to <m>180°</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q3</cell>
                                    <cell><m>180°</m> to <m>270°</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                                </row>
                                <row>
                                    <cell>Q4</cell>
                                    <cell><m>270°</m> to <m>360°</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                                    <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                                </row>
                            </tabular>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Using your table, in which quadrants is the ratio <m>\dfrac{y}{x}</m> positive? When is it negative? When is it undefined?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>\dfrac{y}{x}</m> is positive when <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> have the same sign: in Q1 (both <m>+</m>) and Q3 (both <m>-</m>). It is negative in Q2 (<m>x-</m>, <m>y+</m>) and Q4 (<m>x+</m>, <m>y-</m>). It is undefined when <m>x = 0</m>, i.e. at <m>90°</m> and <m>270°</m>.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <!-- TODO: Add a JSXGraph interactive showing a point dragging around the full unit circle,
         displaying the current angle (0°–360°) and the live values of sin θ, cos θ, and tan θ
         with colour-coding to show when each is positive or negative. -->

    <figure xml:id="fig-cast-diagram">
        <caption>The CAST rule: the bold letter in each quadrant indicates which ratios are positive there.</caption>
        <image width="55%">
            <shortdescription>Unit circle divided into four shaded quadrants. Q1 labelled A (all positive), Q2 labelled S (sine positive), Q3 labelled T (tangent positive), Q4 labelled C (cosine positive).</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\rr}{2.2}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\lpos}{0.62*\rr}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\axlen}{1.25*\rr}

                    % Shade the four quadrants
                    \fill[blue!12]   (0,0) -- (\rr,0)  arc (0:90:\rr)   -- cycle;
                    \fill[green!12]  (0,0) -- (0,\rr)  arc (90:180:\rr)  -- cycle;
                    \fill[orange!18] (0,0) -- (-\rr,0) arc (180:270:\rr) -- cycle;
                    \fill[red!12]    (0,0) -- (0,-\rr) arc (270:360:\rr) -- cycle;

                    % Unit circle
                    \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (\rr);

                    % Axes
                    \draw[-&gt;] (-\axlen,0) -- (\axlen,0) node[right] {$x$};
                    \draw[-&gt;] (0,-\axlen) -- (0,\axlen) node[above] {$y$};

                    % CAST labels: bold letter + positive ratio
                    \node[align=center, blue!60!black]   at ( \lpos, \lpos) {\textbf{\large A}\\\footnotesize all $+$};
                    \node[align=center, green!50!black]  at (-\lpos, \lpos) {\textbf{\large S}\\\footnotesize $\sin +$};
                    \node[align=center, orange!70!black] at (-\lpos,-\lpos) {\textbf{\large T}\\\footnotesize $\tan +$};
                    \node[align=center, red!60!black]    at ( \lpos,-\lpos) {\textbf{\large C}\\\footnotesize $\cos +$};

                    % Origin
                    \node[below left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$O$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <table xml:id="cast-rule-table">
        <title>The CAST Rule: which ratios are positive in each quadrant</title>
        <tabular halign="center">
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                <cell>Quadrant</cell>
                <cell>Angles</cell>
                <cell><m>\sin\theta</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\cos\theta</m></cell>
                <cell><m>\tan\theta</m></cell>
                <cell>Memory</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Q1</cell>
                <cell><m>0°</m> to <m>90°</m></cell>
                <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                <cell><alert>A</alert>ll</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Q2</cell>
                <cell><m>90°</m> to <m>180°</m></cell>
                <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                <cell><alert>S</alert>ine</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Q3</cell>
                <cell><m>180°</m> to <m>270°</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                <cell><alert>T</alert>angent</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell>Q4</cell>
                <cell><m>270°</m> to <m>360°</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                <cell><m>+</m></cell>
                <cell><m>-</m></cell>
                <cell><alert>C</alert>osine</cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
        To find the <em>magnitude</em> of a trig ratio for any angle, we use its <term>reference angle</term><mdash/>the acute angle between the terminal side and the <m>x</m>-axis. The trig ratio of <m>\theta</m> has the same magnitude as the trig ratio of its reference angle; the CAST rule supplies the sign. The reference angle <m>\alpha</m> is computed as follows:
        <ul>
            <li><p>Q1: <m>\alpha = \theta</m></p></li>
            <li><p>Q2: <m>\alpha = 180° - \theta</m></p></li>
            <li><p>Q3: <m>\alpha = \theta - 180°</m></p></li>
            <li><p>Q4: <m>\alpha = 360° - \theta</m></p></li>
        </ul>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-reference-angle-q2">
        <caption>The reference angle <m>\alpha</m> for a Q2 angle <m>\theta = 150°</m>. The reference angle is measured between the terminal side and the negative <m>x</m>-axis.</caption>
        <image width="45%">
            <shortdescription>Unit circle showing a radius at 150 degrees in Q2, with the reference angle of 30 degrees marked in orange between the radius and the negative x-axis.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\rr}{2.2}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\ang}{150}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\pxx}{\rr*cos(\ang)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\pyy}{\rr*sin(\ang)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\refpx}{0.45*cos(\ang)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\refpy}{0.45*sin(\ang)}
                    \pgfmathsetmacro{\axlen}{1.25*\rr}
                    % Shade reference angle region
                    \fill[orange!20] (0,0) -- (-\rr,0) arc (180:\ang:\rr) -- cycle;
                    % Unit circle
                    \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (\rr);
                    % Axes
                    \draw[-&gt;] (-\axlen,0) -- (\axlen,0) node[right] {$x$};
                    \draw[-&gt;] (0,-0.4) -- (0,\axlen) node[above] {$y$};
                    % Radius to P
                    \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0,0) -- (\pxx,\pyy);
                    \filldraw[blue!70!black] (\pxx,\pyy) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$P$};
                    % theta arc (0 to 150)
                    \draw[blue!70!black] (0.7,0) arc (0:\ang:0.7);
                    \node[blue!70!black] at (75:1.0) {$\theta$};
                    % alpha arc (terminal side to negative x-axis)
                    \draw[orange!80!black, thick] (\refpx,\refpy) arc (\ang:180:0.45);
                    \node[orange!80!black] at (165:0.68) {$\alpha$};
                    \node[below left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$O$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <insight>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The unit circle definition extends trig ratios to all angles: for any angle <m>\theta</m>, the point on the unit circle is <m>(\cos\theta,\, \sin\theta)</m> and
                        <me>\tan\theta = \frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}.</me>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>CAST rule</term> gives the sign of each ratio by quadrant:
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Q1 (0°–90°): <alert>A</alert>ll positive</p></li>
                            <li><p>Q2 (90°–180°): <alert>S</alert>ine positive</p></li>
                            <li><p>Q3 (180°–270°): <alert>T</alert>angent positive</p></li>
                            <li><p>Q4 (270°–360°): <alert>C</alert>osine positive</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        To evaluate any trig ratio: find the <term>reference angle</term> <m>\alpha</m> (the acute angle to the <m>x</m>-axis), compute the ratio for <m>\alpha</m>, and apply the sign given by the CAST rule.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>
