    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Reading Ratios from a Point in the Second Quadrant</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The point <m>P = \left(-\dfrac{5}{13},\, \dfrac{12}{13}\right)</m> lies on the unit circle. Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle that <m>OP</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Find <m>\cos\theta</m>, <m>\sin\theta</m>, and <m>\tan\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                First, verify that <m>P</m> is on the unit circle:
                <me>\left(-\frac{5}{13}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{12}{13}\right)^2 = \frac{25}{169} + \frac{144}{169} = \frac{169}{169} = 1. \checkmark</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since the coordinates of a point on the unit circle are <m>(\cos\theta, \sin\theta)</m>:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos\theta \amp= -\frac{5}{13}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\sin\theta \amp= \frac{12}{13}</mrow>
                </md>
                Then
                <me>\tan\theta = \frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta} = \frac{12/13}{-5/13} = -\frac{12}{5}.</me>
            </p>
            <p>
                We can confirm using CAST: since <m>x \lt 0</m> and <m>y \gt 0</m>, the point is in Q2, where only sine is positive. Indeed <m>\sin\theta \gt 0</m>, <m>\cos\theta \lt 0</m>, and <m>\tan\theta \lt 0</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
    
    <example>
        <title>Using the Pythagorean Identity with an Obtuse Angle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>\theta</m> is an obtuse angle with <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{3}{5}</m>. Find <m>\cos\theta</m> and <m>\tan\theta</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Apply the Pythagorean Identity:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta \amp= 1</mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos^2\theta \amp= 1 - \left(\frac{3}{5}\right)^2 = 1 - \frac{9}{25} = \frac{16}{25}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\cos\theta \amp= \pm\frac{4}{5}</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Since <m>\theta</m> is obtuse it lies in Q2, where the CAST rule tells us cosine is negative. So
                <me>\cos\theta = -\frac{4}{5}.</me>
                Then
                <me>\tan\theta = \frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta} = \frac{3/5}{-4/5} = -\frac{3}{4}.</me>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Finding All Angles with a Given Cosine</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A point on the unit circle corresponds to an angle of <m>300^\circ</m>. 
            </p>
            <p>
                Find:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The coordinates of the point on the unit circle corresponding to an angle of <m>300^\circ</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The sine, cosine, and tangent of <m>300^\circ</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The coordinates of the point on the unit circle corresponding to an angle of <m>300^\circ</m> are <m>\left(\frac{1}{2}, -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)</m>.
                    </p>
                    
                </li>
                <li>
                     <p> 
                        Therefore, we can determine the sine, cosine, and tangent as follows:
                            <m>\sin 300^\circ = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\cos 300^\circ = \frac{1}{2}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\tan 300^\circ = \frac{\sin 300^\circ}{\cos 300^\circ} = -\sqrt{3}</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example> 
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all angles <m>\theta</m> in the interval <m>[0°,\, 360°]</m> such that <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The reference angle <m>\alpha</m> satisfies <m>\cos\alpha = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>. Since <m>\cos 60° = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>, we have <m>\alpha = 60°</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                By the CAST rule, <m>\cos\theta</m> is negative in Q2 and Q3. Using the reference angle formulas:
                <ul>
                    <li><p>Q2: <m>\theta = 180° - 60° = 120°</m></p></li>
                    <li><p>Q3: <m>\theta = 180° + 60° = 240°</m></p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                The solutions are <m>\theta = 120°</m> and <m>\theta = 240°</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-cos-two-solutions">
                <caption>Both solutions in <m>[0°, 360°]</m>. Points <m>P_1</m> and <m>P_2</m> both have <m>x</m>-coordinate <m>-\tfrac{1}{2}</m> (dashed line) and share a reference angle of <m>60°</m>.</caption>
                <image width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>Unit circle with radii at 120 degrees (blue) and 240 degrees (red), symmetric about the x-axis, each with a 60-degree reference angle arc at the negative x-axis.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\rr}{2.2}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\angA}{120}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\angB}{240}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\pAx}{\rr*cos(\angA)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\pAy}{\rr*sin(\angA)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\pBx}{\rr*cos(\angB)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\pBy}{\rr*sin(\angB)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\refApx}{0.45*cos(\angA)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\refApy}{0.45*sin(\angA)}
                            \pgfmathsetmacro{\axlen}{1.25*\rr}
                            % Unit circle and axes
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) circle (\rr);
                            \draw[-&gt;] (-\axlen,0) -- (\axlen,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;] (0,-\axlen) -- (0,\axlen) node[above] {$y$};
                            % Dashed symmetry line
                            \draw[dashed,gray!60] (\pAx,\pAy) -- (\pBx,\pBy);
                            % Radii
                            \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (0,0) -- (\pAx,\pAy);
                            \draw[red!70!black, thick] (0,0) -- (\pBx,\pBy);
                            % Reference angle arcs (60 degrees each, near negative x-axis)
                            \draw[orange!80!black, thick] (\refApx,\refApy) arc (\angA:180:0.45);
                            \node[orange!70!black, font=\small] at (150:0.72) {$60°$};
                            \draw[orange!80!black, thick] (-0.45,0) arc (180:\angB:0.45);
                            \node[orange!70!black, font=\small] at (210:0.72) {$60°$};
                            % Points
                            \filldraw[blue!70!black] (\pAx,\pAy) circle (2.5pt)
                                node[above left] {$P_1\ (120°)$};
                            \filldraw[red!70!black] (\pBx,\pBy) circle (2.5pt)
                                node[below left] {$P_2\ (240°)$};
                            \node[below left, font=\small] at (0,0) {$O$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>An Obtuse Angle in a Regular Tetrahedron</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In a regular tetrahedron (a solid with four equilateral triangular faces), the angle between two adjacent face normals satisfies <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{1}{3}</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-tetrahedron">
                <caption>A regular tetrahedron <m>ABCD</m>. Two adjacent faces (shaded) share a common edge; the angle <m>\theta</m> between their outward normals satisfies <m>\cos\theta = -\frac{1}{3}</m>.</caption>
                <image width="40%">
                    <shortdescription>Wireframe of a regular tetrahedron ABCD with two adjacent triangular faces shaded in blue and orange.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
                            % Vertices (2D projection of a regular tetrahedron)
                            \coordinate (A) at (-1.8,  0.2);   % left base
                            \coordinate (B) at ( 1.8,  0.2);   % right base
                            \coordinate (C) at ( 0.3, -1.1);   % front base
                            \coordinate (D) at ( 0,    2.4);   % apex

                            % Shade two adjacent faces sharing edge DB
                            \fill[blue!15]   (D) -- (A) -- (B) -- cycle;
                            \fill[orange!20] (D) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

                            % Apex edges
                            \draw[thick] (D) -- (A);
                            \draw[thick] (D) -- (B);
                            \draw[thick] (D) -- (C);
                            % Base edges (AC hidden)
                            \draw[thick]        (A) -- (B);
                            \draw[thick]        (B) -- (C);
                            \draw[dashed,gray]  (A) -- (C);

                            % Vertex labels
                            \node[above]       at (D) {$D$};
                            \node[above left]  at (A) {$A$};
                            \node[above right] at (B) {$B$};
                            \node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Use a calculator to find <m>\theta</m> to one decimal place.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Verify that this angle is obtuse and explain why this makes geometric sense.</p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <me>\theta = \arccos\!\left(-\frac{1}{3}\right) \approx 109.5°.</me>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{1}{3} \lt 0</m>, the CAST rule tells us <m>\theta</m> is in Q2 or Q3. The <m>\arccos</m> function always returns a value in <m>[0°, 180°]</m>, so <m>\theta</m> is in Q2 and is therefore obtuse.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            This makes geometric sense: the faces of a regular tetrahedron are tilted outward from one another, opening past a right angle, so we expect the angle between adjacent face normals to exceed <m>90°</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>


    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise xml:id="signs-and-quadrants">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each angle, state which quadrant it lies in and give the sign (<m>+</m> or <m>-</m>) of <m>\sin\theta</m>, <m>\cos\theta</m>, and <m>\tan\theta</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\theta = 150°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\theta = 210°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\theta = 315°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\theta = 95°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\theta = 260°</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\theta = 340°</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Q2: <m>\sin\theta +</m>, <m>\cos\theta -</m>, <m>\tan\theta -</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Q3: <m>\sin\theta -</m>, <m>\cos\theta -</m>, <m>\tan\theta +</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Q4: <m>\sin\theta -</m>, <m>\cos\theta +</m>, <m>\tan\theta -</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Q2: <m>\sin\theta +</m>, <m>\cos\theta -</m>, <m>\tan\theta -</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Q3: <m>\sin\theta -</m>, <m>\cos\theta -</m>, <m>\tan\theta +</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Q4: <m>\sin\theta -</m>, <m>\cos\theta +</m>, <m>\tan\theta -</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ratios-from-point">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Each of the following points lies on the unit circle. Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle that <m>OP</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. Find <m>\sin\theta</m>, <m>\cos\theta</m>, and <m>\tan\theta</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>P = \left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\; \dfrac{1}{2}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>P = \left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\; -\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>P = \left(0,\; -1\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>P = \left(\dfrac{5}{13},\; -\dfrac{12}{13}\right)</m></p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Q2: <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>, <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{1}{2}</m>, <m>\tan\theta = -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}} = -\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{3}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Q3: <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>, <m>\sin\theta = -\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>, <m>\tan\theta = 1</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\theta = 270°</m>: <m>\cos\theta = 0</m>, <m>\sin\theta = -1</m>, <m>\tan\theta</m> is undefined.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Q4: <m>\cos\theta = \dfrac{5}{13}</m>, <m>\sin\theta = -\dfrac{12}{13}</m>, <m>\tan\theta = -\dfrac{12}{5}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="pythag-identity-cast">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An angle <m>\theta</m> satisfies <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{7}{25}</m>.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>In which quadrants could <m>\theta</m> lie? Explain your reasoning.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Use the Pythagorean Identity to find the possible values of <m>\sin\theta</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>For each case, find <m>\tan\theta</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>If it is also known that <m>\sin\theta \gt 0</m>, state the quadrant and the exact values of all three ratios.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Cosine is negative in Q2 and Q3, so <m>\theta</m> could lie in either of those quadrants.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <md>
                                    <mrow>\sin^2\theta \amp= 1 - \left(\frac{7}{25}\right)^2 = 1 - \frac{49}{625} = \frac{576}{625}</mrow>
                                    <mrow>\sin\theta \amp= \pm\frac{24}{25}</mrow>
                                </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>If <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{24}{25}</m> (Q2): <m>\tan\theta = \dfrac{24/25}{-7/25} = -\dfrac{24}{7}</m>.</p>
                            <p>If <m>\sin\theta = -\dfrac{24}{25}</m> (Q3): <m>\tan\theta = \dfrac{-24/25}{-7/25} = \dfrac{24}{7}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\sin\theta \gt 0</m> places <m>\theta</m> in Q2, so <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{7}{25}</m>, <m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{24}{25}</m>, <m>\tan\theta = -\dfrac{24}{7}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="find-all-angles">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all angles <m>\theta</m> in the interval <m>[0°,\, 360°]</m> satisfying each equation.
                    <ol marker="(a)" cols="2">
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\tan\theta = -1</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>\sin\theta = -\dfrac{1}{2}</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Reference angle <m>45°</m>; sine positive in Q1 and Q2: <m>\theta = 45°</m> or <m>\theta = 135°</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Reference angle <m>45°</m>; tangent negative in Q2 and Q4: <m>\theta = 135°</m> or <m>\theta = 315°</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Reference angle <m>30°</m>; cosine negative in Q2 and Q3: <m>\theta = 150°</m> or <m>\theta = 210°</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Reference angle <m>30°</m>; sine negative in Q3 and Q4: <m>\theta = 210°</m> or <m>\theta = 330°</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="q3-all-ratios">
            <title>A Point in the Third Quadrant</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A point <m>P</m> on the unit circle satisfies <m>\sin\theta = -\dfrac{5}{13}</m> and lies in the third quadrant.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find <m>\cos\theta</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>\tan\theta</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Write down the exact coordinates of <m>P</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>What is the reference angle for <m>\theta</m>? Leave your answer as an exact expression.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\cos^2\theta = 1 - \dfrac{25}{169} = \dfrac{144}{169}</m>. In Q3, cosine is negative, so <m>\cos\theta = -\dfrac{12}{13}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>\tan\theta = \dfrac{-5/13}{-12/13} = \dfrac{5}{12}</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p><m>P = \left(-\dfrac{12}{13},\; -\dfrac{5}{13}\right)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The reference angle is the acute angle <m>\alpha</m> with <m>\sin\alpha = \dfrac{5}{13}</m> and <m>\cos\alpha = \dfrac{12}{13}</m>, so <m>\alpha = \sin^{-1}\!\left(\dfrac{5}{13}\right)</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="applied-surveying">
            <title>An Obtuse Angle of Inclination</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A surveyor records an angle of inclination of <m>110°</m> for a hillside.
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Without a calculator, state which of <m>\sin 110°</m>, <m>\cos 110°</m>, and <m>\tan 110°</m> are positive and which are negative. Use the CAST rule to justify your answer.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the reference angle for <m>110°</m> and use it to evaluate <m>\sin 110°</m>, <m>\cos 110°</m>, and <m>\tan 110°</m> correct to 3 significant figures.</p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p><m>110°</m> is in Q2. By CAST, only sine is positive in Q2. So <m>\sin 110° \gt 0</m>, <m>\cos 110° \lt 0</m>, <m>\tan 110° \lt 0</m>.</p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Reference angle: <m>\alpha = 180° - 110° = 70°</m>.
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\sin 110° \amp= \sin 70° \approx 0.940</mrow>
                                <mrow>\cos 110° \amp= -\cos 70° \approx -0.342</mrow>
                                <mrow>\tan 110° \amp= -\tan 70° \approx -2.75</mrow>
                            </md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
                </answer>
            </exercise>  
    </exercises> 

